## Why This Is Asked
Explores practical dynamic approximate all-pairs shortest paths (APSP) with shared structure; tests understanding of trade-offs between update costs and query time in batch-dynamic settings.

## Key Concepts
- Batch-dynamic graphs with nonnegative edge weights
- Hub-based distance sketches for approximate APSP
- Amortized analysis across multiple query pairs
- Trade-offs between approximation accuracy ε and computational efficiency
- Incremental shortest path algorithms

## Code Example
```javascript
// Hub-based distance approximation: dist(u,v) ≈ min_h∈H [dist(u,h) + dist(h,v)]

class HubBasedAPSP {
  constructor(graph, epsilon) {
    this.k = Math.ceil((1/epsilon) * Math.log(graph.n));
    this.hubs = this.selectHubs(graph);
    this.distances = new Map(); // hub -> vertex distances
  }

  updateBatch(edges) {
    for (const hub of this.hubs) {
      this.distances.set(hub, this.incrementalDijkstra(hub, edges));
    }
  }

  query(u, v) {
    let minDist = Infinity;
    for (const hub of this.hubs) {
      const dist = this.distances.get(hub).get(u) + 
                   this.distances.get(hub).get(v);
      minDist = Math.min(minDist, dist);
    }
    return minDist;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests sliding-window frequency tracking with fixed memory and per-element updates, a common real-time pattern in fraud/rate-limiting use cases. It also shows practical data-structure trade-offs for beginners.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window with fixed memory
- Circular buffer + hashmap for frequencies
- Per-element O(1) updates; batch O(B)
- Threshold tracking to avoid full scans
- Choosing W, r, B depends on latency and memory constraints

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingWindowCounter {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.buf = new Array(W);
    this.head = 0;
    this.size = 0;
    this.freq = new Map();
    this.crossed = 0; // number of IDs currently crossing the threshold
  }
  processBatch(batch, r) {
    for (const id of batch) {
      // increment
      const c = this.freq.get(id) || 0;
      const nc = c + 1;
      this.freq.set(id, nc);
      if (nc === r) this.crossed++;
      // push to circular buffer
      const idx = (this.head + this.size) % this.W;
      this.buf[idx] = id;
      if (this.size < this.W) {
        this.size++;
      } else {
        // evict oldest
        const old = this.buf[this.head];
        this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.W;
        const co = this.freq.get(old) || 0;
        const nco = co - 1;
        if (nco <= 0) this.freq.delete(old);
        else this.freq.set(old, nco);
        if (co >= r && nco < r) this.crossed--;
      }
    }
    return this.crossed > 0;
  }
}
```

